1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
It holds the things that hold our world together.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:12,000
As American as apple pie and baseball.

3
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:18,000
This arsenal of attaching implements is the place we go when things fall apart.

4
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:24,000
Now, The Hardware Store on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
It has been part of the American landscape since before the Civil War.

6
00:00:49,920 --> 00:01:00,240
In today's 14,300 modern hardware stores, you can find anything for inside and outside the home.

7
00:01:01,260 --> 00:01:04,540
From kitchen faucets to barbecue grills.

8
00:01:05,940 --> 00:01:09,640
Some of today's hardware giants stock over 40,000 different items.

9
00:01:11,060 --> 00:01:12,840
But what were the original products?

10
00:01:13,600 --> 00:01:15,520
What is hardware in its purest form?

11
00:01:15,521 --> 00:01:22,760
To find the answer, we've come to Placerville True Value Hardware in Placerville, California.

12
00:01:23,900 --> 00:01:29,140
Originally opened in 1856, it is one of the oldest hardware stores in America.

13
00:01:30,380 --> 00:01:35,600
Outposts like this supplied the raw materials that helped build our first ranches, farms, and towns.

14
00:01:40,740 --> 00:01:43,600
Surely here we can find out what real hardware is.

15
00:01:43,601 --> 00:01:49,800
well hardware stores you know grew out of sort of the general store concept where you basically

16
00:01:49,801 --> 00:01:54,260
had three types of goods you had you had soft lines which was like clothing and textile you

17
00:01:54,261 --> 00:02:00,980
had food then you had hard lines and hard lines is basically how we got hardware john cotter who's

18
00:02:00,981 --> 00:02:07,020
the founder of true value said if you can't eat it wear it or fold it it's hardware

19
00:02:07,021 --> 00:02:13,780
historians and anthropologists claim that the granddaddy of all tools

20
00:02:13,781 --> 00:02:20,140
is the hammer if this is true then surely the oldest hardware implement must be the nail

21
00:02:20,141 --> 00:02:31,540
right well sort of the first nails were called trennels or tree nails nails made from trees

22
00:02:32,180 --> 00:02:36,660
before we can talk about the metal nail we must first pay homage to its wooden predecessor

23
00:02:36,661 --> 00:02:39,140
Her name is Peg.

24
00:02:41,360 --> 00:02:45,500
The first pegs, or dowels, were simple hand-carved lengths of wood.

25
00:02:46,880 --> 00:02:50,360
Later, specific tools called peg cutters were designed to produce them.

26
00:02:52,820 --> 00:02:58,120
Rough blanks were pounded through sharp-edged cylinders to produce pegs of uniform length and diameter.

27
00:03:00,300 --> 00:03:03,680
Pegs are usually fashioned from wood that is harder than the wood they will join.

28
00:03:06,660 --> 00:03:09,100
What makes a peg joint so strong?

29
00:03:09,101 --> 00:03:11,000
Compression.

30
00:03:11,001 --> 00:03:17,160
Pilot holes drilled to accept pegs are boards slightly smaller than the peg's outer diameter.

31
00:03:17,161 --> 00:03:19,900
When the larger peg is driven into the smaller hole,

32
00:03:19,901 --> 00:03:24,340
the wood fibers must compress to allow the peg to enter.

33
00:03:24,341 --> 00:03:26,380
When glue is added to this equation,

34
00:03:26,381 --> 00:03:32,380
the resulting rust-proof bond is one of the strongest and longest lasting in wood construction.

35
00:03:32,381 --> 00:03:39,640
The arrival of metal during the Bronze Age marked the beginning of the Hardware Age.

36
00:03:40,480 --> 00:03:42,120
Metal really put the hard in hardware.

37
00:03:43,180 --> 00:03:45,360
And the first of these was the lowly nail.

38
00:03:47,240 --> 00:03:49,440
For want of a nail, a shoe was lost.

39
00:03:49,780 --> 00:03:51,580
For want of a shoe, a horse was lost.

40
00:03:52,120 --> 00:03:54,060
For want of a horse, the battle was lost.

41
00:03:54,620 --> 00:03:57,300
For want of the battle, the war was lost.

42
00:03:57,860 --> 00:03:59,780
Or so the ancient verse goes.

43
00:03:59,781 --> 00:04:05,260
Nails were invented in Rome in approximately the 8th century BC.

44
00:04:06,720 --> 00:04:11,380
Slugs of heated bronze or iron were hammered into shape using special anvils,

45
00:04:11,660 --> 00:04:13,260
which formed the heads and points.

46
00:04:16,220 --> 00:04:19,660
Hand-wrought iron nails, though crude by today's standards,

47
00:04:20,180 --> 00:04:22,300
were high-tech devices in ancient times,

48
00:04:22,900 --> 00:04:25,400
and were sometimes used as a substitute for money.

49
00:04:25,401 --> 00:04:30,800
Unlike pegs, they required no pre-drilled hole.

50
00:04:31,700 --> 00:04:35,880
All that was needed was true aim with a hammer and brute force to drive them.

51
00:04:38,640 --> 00:04:41,180
Nails were made in this same way for hundreds of years.

52
00:04:42,140 --> 00:04:48,920
In England and later in colonial America, forged iron was sold in long, thin shafts called nail rod.

53
00:04:50,060 --> 00:04:53,060
This rod was purchased by nail smiths or nailers.

54
00:04:53,061 --> 00:04:57,380
A good nail-making shop could turn out 2,000 nails a day.

55
00:04:59,800 --> 00:05:08,640
In 1775, Jeremiah Wilkinson developed a process for hand-cutting or shearing nails from flat strips of cold-rolled iron instead of from rods.

56
00:05:10,800 --> 00:05:15,880
Even as cut sheet nails began to catch on, hand-wrought nails continued to be made.

57
00:05:15,881 --> 00:05:25,100
In 1794, Thomas Jefferson's Monticello plantation was suffering from damaged soil

58
00:05:25,101 --> 00:05:26,920
and could not sustain a crop.

59
00:05:29,240 --> 00:05:33,940
Jefferson opened a nail-making shop to produce hand-wrought nails and staffed it with slaves.

60
00:05:35,320 --> 00:05:39,840
The nailery was so profitable that it kept the estate solvent for years

61
00:05:39,841 --> 00:05:41,520
until the soil could be restored.

62
00:05:42,320 --> 00:05:44,940
But almost 30 years before Jefferson's nailery,

63
00:05:45,400 --> 00:05:49,220
in 1768, a Massachusetts inventor, Ezekiel Reed,

64
00:05:49,360 --> 00:05:53,260
had patented the first machine for cutting nails from flat sheets of cold steel.

65
00:05:54,040 --> 00:05:56,340
It could produce a thousand nails per hour.

66
00:05:57,120 --> 00:05:59,700
These cut nails were flat or square in section,

67
00:06:00,280 --> 00:06:02,300
and tapered to a rough chiseled point.

68
00:06:05,880 --> 00:06:08,540
Cut nail technology continued to improve,

69
00:06:08,541 --> 00:06:12,620
and cut nails gradually replaced hand-wrought nails as the industry standard.

70
00:06:14,660 --> 00:06:18,540
But the development of Bessemer steel in the middle of the 19th century

71
00:06:18,541 --> 00:06:23,300
spawned the invention of wire nails that have remained the common nails of today.

72
00:06:24,980 --> 00:06:29,440
In 1851, William Hassel patented the first wire nail machine.

73
00:06:30,700 --> 00:06:32,720
The process has changed little over the years.

74
00:06:38,540 --> 00:06:48,140
The wire is on a large spool and it unwinds and is fed into the nail machine.

75
00:06:48,141 --> 00:06:55,140
And only a very small nib of wire is poking out and at that point what's called a header

76
00:06:55,141 --> 00:07:02,780
comes in and smashes that little tiny piece of wire and that forms the head of the nail.

77
00:07:02,781 --> 00:07:08,020
Then that piece of wire, which now is very long but it has only a head on it, is forced

78
00:07:08,021 --> 00:07:13,400
forward and is snipped off to whatever length nail you're looking for by little snippers

79
00:07:13,401 --> 00:07:20,220
on each side or cutters.

80
00:07:20,221 --> 00:07:23,160
The nail machines have gotten a lot faster.

81
00:07:23,161 --> 00:07:27,380
When machines used to be cutting a couple hundred nails a minute, now some are capable

82
00:07:27,381 --> 00:07:32,720
of 800 and 900, and some of the new technology will cut 1,300 and 1,400 nails a minute.

83
00:07:34,460 --> 00:07:39,200
But are the machine-made round wire nails of today truly better than the cut nails of

84
00:07:39,201 --> 00:07:40,080
the 19th century?

85
00:07:43,920 --> 00:07:49,700
A round nail with its pointy snout and smooth shaft doesn't cut the wood fibers as it is

86
00:07:49,701 --> 00:07:52,840
driven, but rather forces its way in between them.

87
00:07:52,841 --> 00:07:57,240
The hold is provided by friction on two sides.

88
00:07:57,241 --> 00:08:01,140
A square nail with its four-sided shaft and chisel point

89
00:08:01,141 --> 00:08:05,140
cuts across the grain, displacing wood fibers as it goes in.

90
00:08:05,141 --> 00:08:11,340
The resulting bond is formed by all four sides being gripped by the compressing wood fibers.

91
00:08:11,341 --> 00:08:15,940
Though not as easy to mass-produce as smooth round nails,

92
00:08:15,941 --> 00:08:18,940
the cut nails holding power is much greater.

93
00:08:18,941 --> 00:08:24,940
Cut nails are still used in pallet making and in laying hardwood floors.

94
00:08:24,941 --> 00:08:31,940
Two applications where constant vibration calls for extra holding power.

95
00:08:31,941 --> 00:08:37,940
Various improvements have been made to wire nails to increase their holding power.

96
00:08:37,941 --> 00:08:40,940
We do a lot of what are called threaded nails.

97
00:08:40,941 --> 00:08:44,460
as opposed to a nail that is just smooth all the way down,

98
00:08:44,580 --> 00:08:49,060
we put a ring shank and a spiral shank on lots of our different products.

99
00:08:49,760 --> 00:08:51,640
And that adds to the holding power.

100
00:08:54,100 --> 00:09:00,100
The hardware industry estimates that Americans pound in 2 billion pounds of nails each year.

101
00:09:03,660 --> 00:09:08,740
Next, see how a mathematician's water pump changed the world of hardware forever.

102
00:09:10,940 --> 00:09:17,540
In early America, settlers who were moving farther west would burn their houses down in order to recover the nails.

103
00:09:18,860 --> 00:09:21,920
The hardware store will return on Modern Marvels.

104
00:09:22,580 --> 00:09:26,320
We now return to the hardware store on Modern Marvels.

105
00:09:29,860 --> 00:09:36,440
If the nail was a giant leap in hardware technology, then the screw must be seen as a trip to the moon.

106
00:09:36,441 --> 00:09:45,200
The Greek mathematician and philosopher Archimedes is credited with inventing the screw about 2300 years ago.

107
00:09:47,080 --> 00:09:51,020
But what was a philosopher-mathematician doing messing around with hardware?

108
00:09:53,080 --> 00:09:54,880
Archimedes was a hardware guy.

109
00:09:54,881 --> 00:09:59,440
The scientists in the ancient world were philosophers on the one hand,

110
00:09:59,580 --> 00:10:04,140
but they were really kind of do-it-yourself guys.

111
00:10:04,141 --> 00:10:10,140
If you had a problem in wartime, for instance, they designed war machines, all kind of catapults.

112
00:10:10,900 --> 00:10:12,920
All of that was done by people like Archimedes.

113
00:10:13,080 --> 00:10:17,580
So they really spanned the whole range of tinkering.

114
00:10:18,700 --> 00:10:21,540
He was both a man of science, but also a kind of engineer.

115
00:10:22,280 --> 00:10:24,840
He didn't really think of it as a screw in the fastener sense.

116
00:10:25,100 --> 00:10:27,520
He really designed it as a product for marine use.

117
00:10:28,040 --> 00:10:32,960
The screw was really a 45-degree shaft that basically went down to the bottom of the boats

118
00:10:32,961 --> 00:10:36,100
and turned to pump water out of the boats and stop them from sinking.

119
00:10:36,540 --> 00:10:38,580
And that was really the purpose of his design.

120
00:10:39,660 --> 00:10:44,320
Now later on, I think in the 13th century, they saw screws being used in wine presses,

121
00:10:44,520 --> 00:10:49,400
but it really wasn't until the 16th and 17th century that you started seeing screws used and bolts

122
00:10:49,401 --> 00:10:52,900
for hardware purposes, mostly in clocks and locks.

123
00:10:53,160 --> 00:10:59,880
Ultimately, it all comes down to Archimedes having this brainwave and coming up with this shape.

124
00:10:59,881 --> 00:11:07,460
After a few centuries of gestation, Archimedes' brainwave began to appear in metalworking shops.

125
00:11:08,560 --> 00:11:12,600
As with many inventions, some of the first applications were in military equipment.

126
00:11:14,380 --> 00:11:20,560
Around the 16th or 17th century, handmade threaded attachments began to appear on suits of armor.

127
00:11:21,980 --> 00:11:27,860
Breastplates, helmets, and visors were held in place by threaded pins and fastened with crude wingnuts.

128
00:11:28,720 --> 00:11:33,040
But each of these screw and nut combinations represented hours of tedious labor.

129
00:11:35,300 --> 00:11:39,540
In those days, there was no running down to the local True Value or Home Depot

130
00:11:39,541 --> 00:11:42,680
for a value pack of 10 fine-thread machine screws.

131
00:11:44,380 --> 00:11:47,360
When a screw was needed, it had to be made from scratch.

132
00:11:48,380 --> 00:11:52,140
The need for screws grew out of the fact that they were vastly superior to nails

133
00:11:52,141 --> 00:11:54,560
on projects that would undergo repeated vibration.

134
00:11:54,561 --> 00:11:59,700
Every time you use a gun, there was this explosion and it would shake.

135
00:11:59,920 --> 00:12:04,980
And so they had to join it in such a way that it wouldn't get rattled apart.

136
00:12:05,600 --> 00:12:10,480
And it's one of the very early uses I found where screws were used, wood screws.

137
00:12:10,980 --> 00:12:13,960
Because clearly a nail wouldn't do the trick.

138
00:12:14,200 --> 00:12:18,000
You needed something relatively small, but very tight.

139
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:24,180
Even a small screw generates enormous holding power.

140
00:12:24,560 --> 00:12:28,300
A screw's grip is not produced by mere friction as with a nail.

141
00:12:29,340 --> 00:12:35,620
As the screw is driven into the wood, the sharp spiral threads slice progressively deeper into the wood fibers,

142
00:12:36,600 --> 00:12:39,260
forming an interpenetrating mechanical bond.

143
00:12:43,520 --> 00:12:47,940
But this special helix shape was the very reason screws were so hard to produce.

144
00:12:48,400 --> 00:12:51,620
A forged round iron slug was first cast.

145
00:12:51,621 --> 00:12:57,580
Then the threads had to be individually filed by hand, exactly identical in pitch and depth.

146
00:13:02,260 --> 00:13:10,140
By the end of the 17th century, other precision devices such as sextants, compasses, and watches were using threaded hardware.

147
00:13:11,400 --> 00:13:16,260
But it would take another 200 years before an actual screw industry was born.

148
00:13:21,620 --> 00:13:26,300
the mass production of screws was a prosaic invention called the cast iron

149
00:13:26,301 --> 00:13:27,100
butt hinge.

150
00:13:31,060 --> 00:13:36,980
In 1775, two Englishmen discovered a way to make butt or butterfly hinges from

151
00:13:36,981 --> 00:13:37,681
cast iron.

152
00:13:39,220 --> 00:13:43,340
These inexpensive hinges were a vast improvement over strap hinges that had

153
00:13:43,341 --> 00:13:45,700
to be nailed across the entire face of a door.

154
00:13:45,701 --> 00:13:56,260
Butt hinges could be hidden inside the door jamb and were almost invisible when the door was closed.

155
00:13:57,200 --> 00:13:59,540
However, butt hinges cannot be nailed.

156
00:14:00,820 --> 00:14:05,760
The narrow attaching surface of the door's edge requires the holding power of several screws.

157
00:14:06,840 --> 00:14:12,860
More than any device before, this common product drove the competition for the first machine-made screws.

158
00:14:15,700 --> 00:14:23,720
In 1760, Job and William Wyatt of Staffordshire, England, patented the first mechanized method for cutting screws.

159
00:14:26,200 --> 00:14:33,540
This process involved turning the unthreaded slug in a spindle while a stationary cutting tool was guided by a lead screw.

160
00:14:35,240 --> 00:14:39,540
In essence, the Wyatts came up with the first screw-making lathe.

161
00:14:39,541 --> 00:14:47,620
Job and William Wyatt did something which is actually revolutionary in its own right,

162
00:14:47,700 --> 00:14:52,440
quite apart from screws, because they developed the first fully automated factory

163
00:14:52,441 --> 00:14:54,340
where the skill was in the machine.

164
00:14:55,120 --> 00:15:01,500
And they were able to turn out these screws hundreds per minute, I mean, at a very fast rate.

165
00:15:02,840 --> 00:15:07,440
One last innovation was needed before the mass-produced screw of the 18th century

166
00:15:07,441 --> 00:15:10,220
would become the hardware we know by the same name today.

167
00:15:14,280 --> 00:15:17,480
The crowning touch was delivered by a New England mechanic

168
00:15:17,481 --> 00:15:19,060
named Colin Whipple.

169
00:15:21,480 --> 00:15:24,760
One of the things about those screws that would surprise us

170
00:15:24,761 --> 00:15:27,760
if we saw one of those old screws today is they had no points.

171
00:15:28,380 --> 00:15:30,120
And that was because they couldn't figure out

172
00:15:30,121 --> 00:15:32,160
how to make a point on these screws.

173
00:15:32,640 --> 00:15:35,400
Because if you look at a screw, a modern screw,

174
00:15:35,401 --> 00:15:39,140
you realize that the thread goes all the way down into the point.

175
00:15:39,880 --> 00:15:43,860
You can't just take a screw and file a point on it.

176
00:15:43,900 --> 00:15:47,720
You've got to have a way of cutting the thread all the way to zero.

177
00:15:49,660 --> 00:15:53,460
Up until this time, thread cutters were locked in a rigid position

178
00:15:53,461 --> 00:15:56,220
that ran along the straight shaft of the screw blank.

179
00:15:57,560 --> 00:15:59,660
Ripple's machine, with its floating cutting bit,

180
00:16:00,180 --> 00:16:04,140
was able to track the profile of a tapered slug all the way to the tip.

181
00:16:05,400 --> 00:16:12,660
Wipple's revolutionary Gimlet point lathe put America into the lead in the screw industry, where it has stayed ever since.

182
00:16:16,600 --> 00:16:20,140
New types of lathes gave birth to new types of threading techniques.

183
00:16:20,840 --> 00:16:25,440
Today, the threads of screws and bolts are cut all at once, instead of one at a time.

184
00:16:26,400 --> 00:16:28,260
This process is called thread rolling.

185
00:16:28,261 --> 00:16:37,380
Rolling has a stationary die and a moving die, and all it does is roll the threads on.

186
00:16:38,060 --> 00:16:43,700
This process, years and years ago, was done by a screw machine where they cut the threads.

187
00:16:43,960 --> 00:16:48,680
This is a lot less expensive to do. There's less scrap. It's more productive.

188
00:16:51,300 --> 00:16:57,720
By the end of the 19th century, factories were turning out millions of perfectly threaded slot-headed screws and bolts.

189
00:16:58,260 --> 00:17:02,060
But now a new problem began to plague the people who used the screws.

190
00:17:02,600 --> 00:17:04,440
That maddling slot in the head.

191
00:17:05,200 --> 00:17:10,820
Up until this time, all screws have been made with a slot or nick across the face of the head with which to drive it.

192
00:17:11,460 --> 00:17:13,360
But that was about to change.

193
00:17:14,820 --> 00:17:17,840
The slot is fine when you're driving screws by hand.

194
00:17:18,380 --> 00:17:21,460
The problem comes when you start using a power screwdriver.

195
00:17:21,461 --> 00:17:24,360
and the problem is called camming out

196
00:17:24,361 --> 00:17:28,340
which is basically when the slotted end of the screwdriver

197
00:17:28,341 --> 00:17:29,820
slips out of the screw

198
00:17:29,821 --> 00:17:32,920
and either eats up your fingers

199
00:17:32,921 --> 00:17:34,920
or scratches up whatever you're working on

200
00:17:34,921 --> 00:17:36,580
it's a bad thing to happen.

201
00:17:37,720 --> 00:17:42,240
In 1907, a Canadian hardware salesman named Peter Robertson

202
00:17:42,241 --> 00:17:45,860
patented a screw with a recessed socket in place of the slot.

203
00:17:46,700 --> 00:17:49,480
This new screw required a special screwdriver

204
00:17:49,481 --> 00:17:50,600
that would mate with a socket.

205
00:17:51,460 --> 00:17:58,440
This screw and screwdriver combination became so popular that Henry Ford considered using it on his assembly line.

206
00:17:59,520 --> 00:18:05,360
But Robertson and Ford, both stubborn men, could not come to an agreement over the manufacturing rights.

207
00:18:06,180 --> 00:18:10,780
Though the socket screw was superior, Ford continued using slotted screws in his cars.

208
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:18,780
Thirty years later, the need for a power-driven screw that wouldn't cam out was even greater.

209
00:18:21,460 --> 00:18:23,726
A Portland inventor named John P.

210
00:18:23,727 --> 00:18:30,182
Thompson sold the patent for his cruciform head screw to a Portland businessman named Henry P.

211
00:18:30,183 --> 00:18:30,883
Phillips.

212
00:18:31,820 --> 00:18:38,120
Phillips modified the star socket design slightly, and voila, the Phillips head screw was born.

213
00:18:39,300 --> 00:18:44,480
Phillips then popularized it, convinced screw manufacturers to adopt it.

214
00:18:44,481 --> 00:18:49,060
Cadillac was actually the first company to use Phillips screws,

215
00:18:49,700 --> 00:18:51,640
and the rest is history.

216
00:18:51,760 --> 00:18:56,480
It became essentially the machine screw of America

217
00:18:56,481 --> 00:18:59,120
and then later in Europe, throughout the world.

218
00:19:01,300 --> 00:19:05,360
Today, our lives contain infinite applications of threaded material.

219
00:19:06,380 --> 00:19:09,860
Every time we replace a light bulb or turn on the garden hose,

220
00:19:10,420 --> 00:19:13,840
we benefit from that original idea of the tinkering philosopher.

221
00:19:14,480 --> 00:19:17,900
Archimedes, the hardware guy.

222
00:19:23,960 --> 00:19:27,420
Next, we'll look at some of the modern hardware inventions of today,

223
00:19:27,840 --> 00:19:30,140
including those that are powered by gunpowder

224
00:19:30,141 --> 00:19:33,140
and can penetrate concrete and steel.

225
00:19:36,420 --> 00:19:40,820
In 1839, John Erickson invented the first steamship propeller

226
00:19:40,821 --> 00:19:43,140
based on the principle of the Archimedes screw.

227
00:19:44,480 --> 00:19:47,040
The hardware store will return on Modern Marvels.

228
00:19:49,420 --> 00:19:52,960
The 20th century brought new products to the construction trades.

229
00:19:53,940 --> 00:19:58,600
Plywood, plastic, and plaster wallboard created the need for a new breed of fasteners

230
00:19:58,601 --> 00:20:00,600
that could work with these new materials.

231
00:20:02,180 --> 00:20:06,280
The kinds of fasteners and pieces and parts that you can buy in a hardware store

232
00:20:06,281 --> 00:20:11,160
really is much more diverse today than it would have been if you went back 50 years or so.

233
00:20:11,161 --> 00:20:17,240
And so too have houses and building materials and construction concepts changed quite a bit over the years.

234
00:20:18,600 --> 00:20:21,240
The old log cabin, of course, you wanted to hang a picture.

235
00:20:21,360 --> 00:20:24,860
You just took a nail and drove it in there and that sucker would hold.

236
00:20:25,400 --> 00:20:31,560
But then when you get to a plaster wall, the nail's going to pull out slowly over time.

237
00:20:31,580 --> 00:20:34,020
So you need something that would hold.

238
00:20:34,340 --> 00:20:38,800
So they invented these goofy things called a molly bolt and a toggle bolt.

239
00:20:38,801 --> 00:20:45,700
One of them, it's like we used to call it a butterfly bolt because it would be a little thing that you'd put in there

240
00:20:45,701 --> 00:20:48,120
and then when it got on the other side it would spread out.

241
00:20:49,360 --> 00:20:54,940
Butterfly bolts or toggle bolts are used for hanging heavier items such as shelving on plaster walls.

242
00:20:55,840 --> 00:20:58,040
Molly bolts are used for pictures and mirrors.

243
00:20:59,900 --> 00:21:06,780
Molly bolts are typically used to attach something to a jitboard wall where you want to hang your grandmother's picture.

244
00:21:06,781 --> 00:21:12,860
You do a pilot hole and then smaller than the actual diameter of the molly bolt and attach the molly bolt.

245
00:21:13,160 --> 00:21:24,833
When you turn the actual screw attachment, it acts as an axle and then compresses the housing around the molly bolt and pinches the drywall or jib board that you're

246
00:21:24,834 --> 00:21:25,534
using.

247
00:21:25,940 --> 00:21:35,242
And then when you drive the actual machine screw at this point, the butterfly, once it penetrates the sheetrock or jib board, simply opens up and pinches the drywall

248
00:21:35,243 --> 00:21:36,200
at that location.

249
00:21:36,780 --> 00:21:47,200
Plywood chipboard and pressure-treated lumber created a demand for nails that could be driven into these materials without getting bent out of shape.

250
00:21:47,560 --> 00:21:55,100
If you're putting up a deck job and you're driving into pressure-treated lumber, it's very frustrating if you have nail bends.

251
00:21:55,240 --> 00:21:58,140
So you want a nail that's going to drive well into that type of product.

252
00:21:58,420 --> 00:22:04,280
And for that, we use a lot of high-carbon steel, which means it's a very durable wire that we start with.

253
00:22:04,281 --> 00:22:07,200
it can be driven easily into very, very hard materials.

254
00:22:08,980 --> 00:22:11,000
But what about the hardest materials?

255
00:22:11,860 --> 00:22:14,240
What about concrete, rock, and masonry?

256
00:22:15,380 --> 00:22:19,560
Driving into these materials calls for the most powerful of all hardware devices,

257
00:22:19,960 --> 00:22:22,200
a powder-actuated fastener.

258
00:22:23,640 --> 00:22:26,880
The actual shot, or pin, as it is called in the industry,

259
00:22:27,000 --> 00:22:29,520
is actually a hardened steel-type fastener.

260
00:22:30,140 --> 00:22:33,540
And when the cap, which is loaded into the back of the tool, explodes,

261
00:22:33,541 --> 00:22:40,760
is piston driven and the piston drives this pin through whatever you're attaching to the concrete

262
00:22:40,761 --> 00:22:49,920
typically that is used to attach in this day and age metal stud framing to a flat concrete slab

263
00:22:49,921 --> 00:22:56,720
some of the latest powder actuated fasteners can actually drive into half inch steel

264
00:22:56,721 --> 00:23:08,220
There are different kinds of powder actually in the two of its different between say a 22 caliber and a 38 caliber pistol as it were.

265
00:23:08,221 --> 00:23:20,692
And so therefore if the fastener is larger and the subject attachment is a piece of steel channel that needs to be attached to a concrete substrate, you would need a

266
00:23:20,693 --> 00:23:23,640
larger caliber as well as a larger pin.

267
00:23:23,641 --> 00:23:31,360
the evolution of new more powerful more efficient tools has also brought about new designs and

268
00:23:31,361 --> 00:23:39,620
hardware driving the nail has changed because we have nail guns and sort of batteries of nails

269
00:23:39,621 --> 00:23:47,680
they look like bullets almost that are driven pneumatically or mechanically um and and i imagine

270
00:23:47,681 --> 00:23:54,300
and the technology of the nail eventually will change in response to the fact that you're driving it differently.

271
00:23:54,760 --> 00:24:00,420
My favorite new power tool is one that I saw at the hardware store the other day,

272
00:24:00,421 --> 00:24:04,660
and I even talked to the hardware store guy into letting me take it home and try it out.

273
00:24:05,020 --> 00:24:08,800
It's a power screwdriver that looks like something Rambo would use.

274
00:24:10,720 --> 00:24:17,520
This thing has a running sheaf of screws on a paper thing like a machine gun.

275
00:24:17,680 --> 00:24:21,080
You do one screw, and the next one, it shoots right in there.

276
00:24:25,260 --> 00:24:26,760
And it's really fun.

277
00:24:29,400 --> 00:24:32,600
Next, we'll see how the mad scientists of hardware

278
00:24:32,601 --> 00:24:36,100
are replacing conventional fasteners with stuff that sticks.

279
00:24:38,320 --> 00:24:41,880
NASA has developed specialized fasteners called smart bolts

280
00:24:41,881 --> 00:24:44,520
that can tell you how tight or loose they are.

281
00:24:44,521 --> 00:24:48,760
The Hardware Store will return on Modern Marvels.

282
00:24:49,820 --> 00:24:53,820
Even with all the high-tech widgets available at your local hardware store,

283
00:24:54,220 --> 00:24:57,060
there are still some jobs that defy mechanical fastening.

284
00:24:57,720 --> 00:25:03,380
So if you can't nail it, or you can't screw it, chances are you'll have to glue it.

285
00:25:04,460 --> 00:25:08,300
Today, hardware doesn't necessarily mean hardware.

286
00:25:09,640 --> 00:25:13,760
You think nails and screws, but that's not the only way that we fasten stuff anymore.

287
00:25:13,761 --> 00:25:14,622
We have glues.

288
00:25:14,623 --> 00:25:20,100
We have so many different kinds of glue that when I go to the hardware store to buy glue,

289
00:25:20,660 --> 00:25:25,720
I feel like I'm in school again having to read all the instructions on what this will do, what that won't do.

290
00:25:26,660 --> 00:25:28,760
Well, glues are really one of the oldest products.

291
00:25:28,960 --> 00:25:32,020
I mean, it used to be thought that the glue was invented by the Egyptians.

292
00:25:32,260 --> 00:25:33,700
We found evidence of it in Egypt.

293
00:25:34,080 --> 00:25:38,280
But actually about two years ago, there was a discovery in Israel in the Dead Sea area

294
00:25:38,281 --> 00:25:41,220
where they found glue that traced back 8,000 years.

295
00:25:41,221 --> 00:25:43,580
It was hide glue. Hide glue is still in use today.

296
00:25:43,760 --> 00:25:46,400
So it was a good idea then, it's a good idea now.

297
00:25:48,520 --> 00:25:52,780
Ancient hide glue was made by boiling and rendering animal hides and hooves

298
00:25:52,781 --> 00:25:55,880
into a thick paste that hardened when cool and dry.

299
00:25:56,580 --> 00:25:59,800
Other natural substances like tar, tree sap, and wax

300
00:25:59,801 --> 00:26:03,200
were also used by carpenters, carriage builders, and shoemakers.

301
00:26:05,100 --> 00:26:09,680
In the 19th century, chemical concoctions began to replace nature's glue.

302
00:26:09,681 --> 00:26:17,380
At first, there were simple formulas like wood glue, contact cement, and rubber cement.

303
00:26:18,180 --> 00:26:22,120
Then, epoxy made a big hit with its two-part chemical reaction system

304
00:26:22,121 --> 00:26:26,120
that mixed a strong, flexible resin base with a catalyst hardener.

305
00:26:27,220 --> 00:26:30,800
Epoxies were flexible enough to be used in making golf clubs and skis.

306
00:26:32,880 --> 00:26:37,880
And then, in 1951, came the first stuff that could stick anything to anything.

307
00:26:38,820 --> 00:26:41,240
The chemical name is sinoacrylates.

308
00:26:42,580 --> 00:26:45,100
We came to know it as superglue.

309
00:26:45,740 --> 00:26:47,300
It was actually discovered by accident.

310
00:26:48,760 --> 00:26:55,920
Some guys at Eastman Chemical Company in East Tennessee were working with chemical compounds,

311
00:26:56,100 --> 00:26:58,340
and they were trying to figure out what these things would do,

312
00:26:58,440 --> 00:27:02,560
and they put some of this compound in the microscope to look at it,

313
00:27:02,561 --> 00:27:08,920
and when they came to take it out, this is like a $20,000 electron microscope.

314
00:27:09,320 --> 00:27:12,300
When they came to take it out, it wouldn't come out.

315
00:27:12,980 --> 00:27:19,100
It had cemented itself in there, and that's how superglue came along.

316
00:27:21,100 --> 00:27:26,520
By 1958, this cyanoacrylate was being marketed as Eastman 910.

317
00:27:27,340 --> 00:27:32,320
It would come to wear other names such as Crazy Glue, Super Glue, and Black Max.

318
00:27:32,560 --> 00:27:35,700
But what never changed was its strength.

319
00:27:36,260 --> 00:27:40,560
A one-inch square application of this stuff can lift up to a ton without letting go.

320
00:27:41,880 --> 00:27:43,240
How much do you weigh, Wayne?

321
00:27:43,420 --> 00:27:45,580
About 187. Cheating a little bit.

322
00:27:48,920 --> 00:27:54,280
Some advanced adhesive products are used in the construction industry to work along with conventional hardware.

323
00:27:56,180 --> 00:28:00,560
Subsequent to the size of the activities we experienced here in Southern California in 1994,

324
00:28:00,561 --> 00:28:09,520
there were all types of adhesives used to reinforce concrete structural members and lentils

325
00:28:09,521 --> 00:28:14,140
with whatever form of attachment device you were using, redhead concrete anchor or so forth.

326
00:28:15,900 --> 00:28:19,000
Superglue was good for small, high-stress applications,

327
00:28:19,640 --> 00:28:25,280
but its merciless permanent adhesion to anything or any body required cautious application.

328
00:28:26,060 --> 00:28:29,500
For larger jobs that wouldn't be subjected to constant stress,

329
00:28:29,501 --> 00:28:32,320
a less potent adhesive caulking was developed.

330
00:28:33,240 --> 00:28:35,600
It was odorless, safe, and easy to apply.

331
00:28:36,700 --> 00:28:40,660
Where the homeowner once used iron nails, brass nails, or steel nails,

332
00:28:41,040 --> 00:28:42,740
now he could use liquid nails.

333
00:28:43,580 --> 00:28:47,340
Liquid nails is probably one of the products and one of the adhesives

334
00:28:47,341 --> 00:28:52,280
that most people in the building trades and even the hardware business know a lot about.

335
00:28:52,400 --> 00:28:57,400
It is sort of a very all-purpose epoxy that was applied with a caulk gun.

336
00:28:57,401 --> 00:29:01,960
And the manufacturer realized that people were using this product for just about everything.

337
00:29:02,200 --> 00:29:04,760
And they even make this product in a squeeze bottle.

338
00:29:04,880 --> 00:29:09,280
They even make it in a tube that reminds you of a tube you would use to decorate a cake.

339
00:29:11,380 --> 00:29:17,100
Companies like 3M and Manco are working toward the day when they can put screws and nails out of business.

340
00:29:18,600 --> 00:29:26,820
3M looks at alternative methods to the conventional means of screws, rivets, and mechanical fasteners.

341
00:29:26,821 --> 00:29:35,440
What we're looking at are products that replace these materials and make it easy for a consumer or a manufacturer to utilize.

342
00:29:37,420 --> 00:29:42,840
Some of these products use heat-activated technology to chemically weld materials together.

343
00:29:43,620 --> 00:29:49,260
These thermoplastic polymers are rock-hard at room temperature, but become liquid when heated in the applicator.

344
00:29:49,261 --> 00:29:56,980
One of the exciting products that 3M has developed is a hybrid technology called JetWeld.

345
00:29:57,560 --> 00:30:04,720
It takes the speed of a hot melt with the structural strength of an epoxy in a one-part moisture cure urethane.

346
00:30:05,380 --> 00:30:12,320
So you've got the ease of application and the strength to replace materials such as nails and also even white glue.

347
00:30:13,740 --> 00:30:18,480
The very latest sticky stuff is strong enough to replace aircraft hardware.

348
00:30:19,260 --> 00:30:23,760
3M has developed materials that can replace spot welding and rivets.

349
00:30:23,940 --> 00:30:28,160
In many cases, what we're able to do, obviously, in replacing a rivet,

350
00:30:28,380 --> 00:30:30,080
is replace the drilling of the holes.

351
00:30:30,400 --> 00:30:33,340
We can do this with some of our two-part epoxies,

352
00:30:33,740 --> 00:30:36,840
which have the ability to remain flexible

353
00:30:36,841 --> 00:30:41,280
and withstand the environment that a commercial aircraft is placed into.

354
00:30:42,860 --> 00:30:45,700
Even with all the hundreds of glue products out there,

355
00:30:45,701 --> 00:30:51,300
the actual process of how glue works is still somewhat of a mystery, or at least a debate.

356
00:30:53,100 --> 00:30:56,680
Physiochemical theories abound as to what makes the sticky stuff stick.

357
00:30:57,600 --> 00:31:01,520
Is it electrostatic diffusion or molecular strand absorption?

358
00:31:02,600 --> 00:31:07,520
One popular theory being studied suggests that glue acts like chemical Velcro

359
00:31:07,521 --> 00:31:10,080
that hooks the bonded molecules together.

360
00:31:10,081 --> 00:31:18,860
What happens when you give glue a little backbone of canvas or vinyl?

361
00:31:19,460 --> 00:31:22,320
You get that wonderful all-purpose stuff called tape.

362
00:31:25,040 --> 00:31:30,220
Some of the strong tapes that 3M has are what we call very high bond tapes.

363
00:31:31,660 --> 00:31:37,000
Fiber tape, electrical tape, epoxy tape, double-sided tape, heat-proof tape,

364
00:31:37,001 --> 00:31:42,220
And the most popular tape of all, the fabric of our hardware lives, duct tape.

365
00:31:43,480 --> 00:31:48,500
This amazing all-purpose adhesive product is so widely used in so many industries

366
00:31:48,501 --> 00:31:51,200
that it has developed its own cult following.

367
00:31:51,940 --> 00:31:55,760
The gurus of this shticky movement are the clown princes of hardware,

368
00:31:56,360 --> 00:31:58,240
the duct tape guys, Tim and Jim.

369
00:32:01,200 --> 00:32:03,040
Hey, is that refreshing, Jim?

370
00:32:03,600 --> 00:32:06,020
Look at my pop. It's four times as refreshing.

371
00:32:06,021 --> 00:32:07,580
Wow, how'd you make that?

372
00:32:07,660 --> 00:32:08,626
Well, it's simple.

373
00:32:08,627 --> 00:32:12,220
You just stack up four pop cans, duct tape them together like that.

374
00:32:12,320 --> 00:32:17,020
You get your three-foot-long drill bit, drill down through them all, and...

375
00:32:19,140 --> 00:32:29,520
This duct tape duo has written four books and produced a video on the many uses of this all-purpose product.

376
00:32:32,060 --> 00:32:35,680
Duct tape was created for the U.S. military during World War II.

377
00:32:35,681 --> 00:32:39,640
They needed a waterproof tape to keep the moisture out of ammunition cases.

378
00:32:40,200 --> 00:32:44,920
So they enlisted the Johnson & Johnson Permacel Division to come up with this waterproof tape.

379
00:32:45,300 --> 00:32:48,460
And it was kind of based on the cloth medical tapes that they had been making.

380
00:32:48,980 --> 00:32:53,860
Because the stuff was waterproof, everybody referred to it as duct tape.

381
00:32:54,060 --> 00:32:55,620
You know, like water off a duck's back.

382
00:32:56,240 --> 00:32:59,940
And they started figuring out, hey, this is good for a lot more than the ammunition cases.

383
00:33:00,020 --> 00:33:03,400
So they repaired their jeeps with it, their guns, all kinds of stuff.

384
00:33:03,401 --> 00:33:09,000
And then after the war, they started using it to hold the heating and air conditioning duct work together

385
00:33:09,001 --> 00:33:11,080
because the housing industry was booming.

386
00:33:11,380 --> 00:33:13,840
Well, I've bandaged myself with duct tape.

387
00:33:14,740 --> 00:33:21,280
As a journeyman carpenter, there was never time enough to bleed on a construction site.

388
00:33:21,660 --> 00:33:24,140
They actually took care of that when you were off duty.

389
00:33:24,600 --> 00:33:27,380
So it comes in very handy for first aid.

390
00:33:29,080 --> 00:33:30,940
Duct tape is made of three layers.

391
00:33:31,660 --> 00:33:37,120
The cloth mesh in the middle is sandwiched between a waterproof polyethylene coating on one side

392
00:33:37,121 --> 00:33:39,600
and natural rubber adhesive on the other.

393
00:33:40,680 --> 00:33:44,840
It's as strong as nylon tow rope, yet it can be ripped with bare hands.

394
00:33:46,720 --> 00:33:53,320
But despite its multitude of uses, one must pause to wonder, is duct tape really hardware?

395
00:33:54,320 --> 00:33:56,680
Duct tape is almost anti-hardware.

396
00:33:56,681 --> 00:34:03,020
It's for people who don't know what tool to use or how to actually fix something,

397
00:34:03,140 --> 00:34:05,700
and it's a kind of desperate measure.

398
00:34:05,940 --> 00:34:08,900
I think it's all hardware products rolled into one.

399
00:34:09,080 --> 00:34:09,780
That's right.

400
00:34:09,781 --> 00:34:15,760
I admit that I use it, although I try not to use it to do anything serious or permanent.

401
00:34:16,600 --> 00:34:22,060
I wrap up the garbage with it, but it's very good for moving.

402
00:34:22,061 --> 00:34:27,180
but I always feel bad when I see an automobile that's been repaired with duct tape

403
00:34:27,181 --> 00:34:33,400
because it's really the wrong material doing the wrong thing for the wrong reasons

404
00:34:33,401 --> 00:34:38,160
and it represents a kind of loss of knowledge about how to fix things.

405
00:34:39,520 --> 00:34:45,320
Many adhesive makers claim that someday glues and epoxies might eliminate traditional hardware altogether.

406
00:34:46,300 --> 00:34:53,400
But for now, it's hard to imagine a house without nails, screws, bolts, and our trusty roll of duct tape.

